        <div id="post-725" class="post-725 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-silverlight category-tfs">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/tfs-screencasts-part-1-create-a-team-project/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: TFS Screencasts &#8211; Part 1 &#8211; Create a Team Project">TFS Screencasts &#8211; Part 1 &#8211; Create a Team Project</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Aug</span>
            <span class="day">27</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/silverlight/" title="View all posts in Silverlight" rel="category tag">Silverlight</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/tfs/" title="View all posts in TFS" rel="category tag">TFS</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/tfs-screencasts-part-1-create-a-team-project/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on TFS Screencasts &#8211; Part 1 &#8211; Create a Team Project"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="725 /blogs/ryanc/archive/2008/08/27/tfs-screencasts-part-1-create-a-team-project.aspx">No Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>One of the projects I&#8217;m working on for my company, Strategic Data Systems, is our internal Team Foundation Server 2008 solution.&nbsp; I&#8217;m working with some of the guys who head up our Agile Development Center to make sure things fit well with what they are doing.&nbsp; It&#8217;s been difficult to find overlapping free time, so [...]</p>

                    </div>
        </div>
        <!-- /post -->
        
        <!-- post -->
        <div id="post-724" class="post-724 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-software-reviews">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/outlook-2007-does-the-little-things/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Outlook 2007 does the little things">Outlook 2007 does the little things</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Aug</span>
            <span class="day">26</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/software-reviews/" title="View all posts in Software Reviews" rel="category tag">Software Reviews</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/outlook-2007-does-the-little-things/#comments" class="comments"  title="Comment on Outlook 2007 does the little things"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="724 /blogs/ryanc/archive/2008/08/26/outlook-2007-does-the-little-things.aspx">2 Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>People give Microsoft Outlook a lot of crap, but if you&#8217;re willing to think outside the box, you&#8217;ll realize they did all the little things that make life easy.&nbsp; For instance, I rarely use the calendar popup for creating new appointments.&nbsp; No, if I have a meeting next Wednesday, I type &#8220;next wed&#8221; and hit [...]</p>

                    </div>
        </div>
        <!-- /post -->
        
        <!-- post -->
        <div id="post-723" class="post-723 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-sql-server">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/sql-server-2000-vs-2005-lazy-xml-validation/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: SQL Server 2000 vs 2005 Lazy XML Validation">SQL Server 2000 vs 2005 Lazy XML Validation</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Aug</span>
            <span class="day">19</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/sql-server/" title="View all posts in SQL Server" rel="category tag">SQL Server</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/sql-server-2000-vs-2005-lazy-xml-validation/#comments" class="comments"  title="Comment on SQL Server 2000 vs 2005 Lazy XML Validation"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="723 /blogs/ryanc/archive/2008/08/19/sql-server-2000-vs-2005-lazy-xml-validation.aspx">1 Comment</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>Someone was looking to do quick-and-dirty validation of an Xml document inside a SQL Server procedure today.&nbsp; I assumed, and regretted, that he meant 2005, which would have looked like this: declare @xml1 xml&nbsp; declare @xml2 xml&nbsp; set @xml1 = &#8216;&lt;Node1&gt;&lt;Node2 attrib=&#8221;This is it.&#8221; /&gt;&lt;/Node1&gt;&#8217;set @xml2 = &#8216;&lt;Node1&gt;&lt;Node2 /&gt;&lt;/Node1&gt;&#8217;select Col.value(&#8216;count(./@attrib)&#8217;, &#8216;int&#8217;) as countfrom @xml1.nodes(&#8216;Node1/Node2&#8242;) as [...]</p>

                    </div>
        </div>
        <!-- /post -->
        
        <!-- post -->
        <div id="post-722" class="post-722 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-uncategorized">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/installation-guides/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Installation Guides">Installation Guides</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Aug</span>
            <span class="day">6</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/uncategorized/" title="View all posts in Uncategorized" rel="category tag">Uncategorized</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/installation-guides/#comments" class="comments"  title="Comment on Installation Guides"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="722 /blogs/ryanc/archive/2008/08/06/installation-guides.aspx">2 Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>This evening I completed my first split-server (or dual server as it&#8217;s called in the guide) installation of Team Foundation Server 2008.&nbsp; I&#8217;ve installed TFS a few times now in single-server mode which is pretty crazy easy when you consider what you get (way more than SVN or CVS by themselves).&nbsp; Throughout the entire process [...]</p>

                    </div>
        </div>
        <!-- /post -->
        
        <!-- post -->
        <div id="post-721" class="post-721 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-virtual-pc">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/reason-29-for-using-virtual-machines/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Reason #29 for using Virtual Machines">Reason #29 for using Virtual Machines</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Aug</span>
            <span class="day">6</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/virtual-pc/" title="View all posts in Virtual PC" rel="category tag">Virtual PC</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2008/08/reason-29-for-using-virtual-machines/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on Reason #29 for using Virtual Machines"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="721 /blogs/ryanc/archive/2008/08/06/reason-29-for-using-virtual-machines.aspx">No Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>Today I blew up a server.&nbsp; Couldn&#8217;t figure something out so I pushed every button I could find, flipped every switch and changed every setting.&nbsp; Nothing.&nbsp; So you know what I did, I deleted it, copied over a new Sysprepped Hard Drive and had a clean slate in about 5 minutes. I figured out my [...]</p>

